# Chapter Ten: Among the Branches

The night settled over the upper woods like a heavy velvet blanket, plunging the clearing into a profound, isolated quiet. Inside the main cabin, the warmth of the stone fireplace and the easy laughter of Sindhura and Mandhira over a board game had lulled the family into a deep, domestic comfort. Mrs. Kashyap watched her son from across the room, her heart finally at ease seeing the heavy, unrelenting armor slip from his shoulders.

When the clock ticked past midnight, the family retired to their rooms, leaving the clearing wrapped in the gentle rustle of pine needles and the distant, soothing hum of the forest.

Across the wooden pathway, the treehouse stood bathed in pale moonlight.

Ira stood on the wrap-around balcony of the upper loft, her arms crossed lightly over her chest as she stared out at the sprawling, misty valley below. The air was crisp, biting just enough to make her shiver, but the sheer, breathtaking beauty of the night kept her rooted to the spot.

A soft click echoed behind her as the door to the adjoining room opened. Footsteps padded quietly across the wooden deck, and a moment later, a heavy, familiar scent of cedar and rain wrapped around her.

Shiv stepped out, holding two mugs of hot coffee. He didn't say a word at first; he simply walked up beside her at the railing, extending one of the mugs toward her. His fingers brushed against hers—a fleeting contact that sent a sudden, sharp jolt straight up her arm.

"You're going to catch a cold standing out here without a jacket," Shiv said softly, his voice a low, gravelly hum in the quiet night.

Ira took the mug, wrapping her cold hands around the warm ceramic. "I couldn't sleep. Up here... it feels like we're completely disconnected from the rest of the world. No boardrooms, no enemies, no shadows."

Shiv leaned his forearms against the wooden railing, turning his head to look at her profile bathed in the silver moonlight. The easy, playful host persona he had worn for his family downstairs was completely gone, replaced by the raw, unfiltered intensity that belonged only to her.

"Do you want it to be real?" Shiv asked quietly, his dark gray eyes searching her face with an aching vulnerability. "This life? Just... us, away from everything?"

Ira turned to face him, her breath catching in her throat. The question hung between them, heavy with years of built-up walls, unspoken fears, and a magnetic pull neither of them could fight anymore.

"It's easy to want a fantasy when reality is trying to destroy us, Shiv," Ira replied, her voice trembling slightly. "But you and I... we live in the crossfire. You carry an empire on your shoulders, and I carry a target on my back. How are we supposed to pretend we can just step out of the storm?"

Shiv pushed away from the railing, closing the distance between them until the space heater of his body heat chased away the chill of the mountain air. He didn't offer empty reassurances. He didn't lie and tell her it would be easy. Instead, he reached out, his warm, calloused fingers gently cupping the side of her face, his thumb tracing the curve of her cheekbone with a fierce, possessive tenderness.

"I've spent my entire life protecting things," Shiv whispered, his voice cracking with a raw edge of emotion that terrified and seduced her all at once. "My family, my legacy, my borders. I built walls so thick nothing could ever touch me. But the moment you walked into my life, you didn't break down my walls, Ira... you made me realize I was already living in a cage."

Ira’s heart hammered frantically against her ribs. The air felt thin, stolen away not by the altitude, but by the sheer weight of his gaze. "Shiv..." she breathed out, her hands coming up to rest against the solid wall of his chest, feeling the frantic, heavy beat of his heart beneath his shirt.

"I can't promise you a safe, ordinary life," Shiv murmured, his eyes dropping to her lips before rising back to meet her intense, unblinking stare. "I can't promise the world won't try to tear us apart. But I am promising you this—as long as I am breathing, no one will ever touch you. And every single wall I have left... I am tearing down for you."

The last remnants of Ira's defenses shattered. The fear, the hesitation, the logic of their dangerous world—it all melted away into the dark, fragrant night. She didn't answer him with words. Instead, she rose up slightly on her tiptoes, closing the microscopic gap between them.

When his lips met hers, it wasn't a tentative exploration. It was deep, deliberate, and consuming—a quiet storm of long-repressed longing. Shiv’s arm banded tightly around her waist, lifting her effortlessly as he pulled her flush against him, pressing her back against the sturdy timber pillar of the loft.

Ira gasped softly against his mouth, her fingers tangling into the thick hair at the nape of his neck, pulling him closer as the kiss deepened into something breathless and wild. Every touch carried the weight of a dozen unspoken conversations.

Shiv groaned softly, his hand sliding up from her waist to cup the back of her head, his fingers threading through her locks as he tilted her face upward, demanding more, taking everything she had to offer. His lips burned a trail from her mouth down along the sensitive curve of her jaw, lingering against the hollow of her throat where her pulse hammered wildly.

Ira arched into his touch, her breath hitching as a shiver that had nothing to do with the mountain cold rippled through her spine. Her hands slid down from his chest to grip the lapels of his jacket, pulling him tighter, needing the solid, unyielding reality of him to anchor her.

When he lifted his head to look at her again, his dark eyes were dilated and burning with an undisguised hunger. He slowly set her back down, but his hands didn't leave her body; his palms remained splayed flat against her lower back, holding her flush against him so she could feel every frantic beat of his pulse matching her own.

"I have dreamed about this every single day since I saw you fight those men," Shiv murmured, his voice rough, brushing his lips against hers once more in a slow, agonizingly tender kiss.

Ira's hands pressed firmly against his chest, her breath coming in shallow, unsteady gasps as the sudden, overwhelming closeness of a man—and the raw, consuming power of her own desire—caught up with her. "I... I think we should stop," she whispered, her voice barely audible over the wind rustling through the pines.

Shiv froze, his dark eyes searching hers, recognizing the sudden flutter of panic and untamed emotion in her gaze. Slowly, reluctantly, his arms unwrapped from around her waist, giving her the space she needed while his thumb brushed one last time across her cheek.

"Alright," Shiv murmured softly, his voice thick with restraint as he stepped back. "We stop."
